CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ braced base element: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ empty list: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ copy-list: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [dcl.init.aggr]/4-5: in `E x{{1}, 2}' the base element A is
aggregate-initialized from {1}: a from 1, c from its default member
initializer 9.  The front end assigned only the listed values to the
base's members, leaving c indeterminate, and initialized `E y{}' by E's
implicit default constructor alone, leaving a and e indeterminate (bin53).
A base element now initializes each of its members from the list, its
default member initializer or zero; an empty list value-initializes
([dcl.init.general]/9.1: zero first).  The test is C++17 (an aggregate
with a base).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails
only its WRONG assertion.
